					</div>

					<div class="prose">
						<p>
							The deeper contribution is the defect taxonomy. Instead of "MAS
							failed," the authors trace <em>where</em> in the graph failure
							enters — and two of the three defect classes are caused by the
							collaboration itself.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="cards">
						<div class="card">
							<svg class="mini" viewBox="0 0 220 90" role="img" aria-label="Pipeline of three agents with the middle node highlighted as defective.">
								<line x1="58" y1="40" x2="96" y2="40" stroke="#aebac4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<line x1="124" y1="40" x2="162" y2="40" stroke="#aebac4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<circle cx="44" cy="40" r="14" fill="#e8f0fa" stroke="#9ec5f4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<circle cx="110" cy="40" r="14" fill="#fbe3e1" stroke="#d03b3b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
								<circle cx="176" cy="40" r="14" fill="#e8f0fa" stroke="#9ec5f4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<text x="110" y="76" font-size="11" fill="#596774" text-anchor="middle">the critical agent is too weak</text>
							</svg>
							<h3>Node-level defect</h3>
							<p>
								The bottleneck agent simply is not capable enough. The fix is
								surgical: their confidence-weighted critical-path tracing found
								which single agent in a heterogeneous 3-solver AIME debate to
								upgrade — and picked optimally in both directions (upgrade and
								safe downgrade).
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="card">
							<svg class="mini" viewBox="0 0 220 90" role="img" aria-label="Pipeline of three agents with the connecting edge highlighted as defective.">
								<line x1="58" y1="40" x2="96" y2="40" stroke="#d03b3b" stroke-width="3.5"/>
								<line x1="124" y1="40" x2="162" y2="40" stroke="#aebac4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<circle cx="44" cy="40" r="14" fill="#e8f0fa" stroke="#9ec5f4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<circle cx="110" cy="40" r="14" fill="#e8f0fa" stroke="#9ec5f4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<circle cx="176" cy="40" r="14" fill="#e8f0fa" stroke="#9ec5f4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<text x="110" y="76" font-size="11" fill="#596774" text-anchor="middle">upstream floods downstream</text>
							</svg>
							<h3>Edge-level defect</h3>
							<p>
								"Overzealous teammates": in Self-Collaboration, the analyst and
								tester agents inject unnecessary corner cases that overload the
								coder — inducing failures the solo coder never makes. The
								collaboration <em>creates</em> the error.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="card">
							<svg class="mini" viewBox="0 0 220 90" role="img" aria-label="Pipeline of three agents where an early error propagates along the whole path.">
								<line x1="58" y1="40" x2="96" y2="40" stroke="#d03b3b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
								<line x1="124" y1="40" x2="162" y2="40" stroke="#d03b3b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
								<circle cx="44" cy="40" r="14" fill="#fbe3e1" stroke="#d03b3b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
								<circle cx="110" cy="40" r="14" fill="#e8f0fa" stroke="#9ec5f4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<circle cx="176" cy="40" r="14" fill="#e8f0fa" stroke="#9ec5f4" stroke-width="2"/>
								<text x="110" y="76" font-size="11" fill="#596774" text-anchor="middle">early loss becomes unrecoverable</text>
							</svg>
							<h3>Path-level defect</h3>
							<p>
								"Lost in summarization": in a math debate, a <em>correct</em>
								early-round solution was dropped during summarization before
								hand-off — and no downstream agent could ever recover it. Small
								context dilution snowballs into irreversible failure.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="prose">
						<p>
							The proposal follows from the diagnosis: don't pick an architecture,
							pick per request. A cheap LLM rater routes hard requests to MAS
							(≈2% better than pure MAS at half its cost on a GSM8K+AIME mix), and
							a cascade tries SAS first, verifies, and escalates only on failure.
						</p>
					</div>

