			<!-- ============ PAPER 3: Tran & Kiela ============ -->
			<section class="block alt" id="budget">
				<div class="wrap">
					<div class="section-head">
						<p class="eyebrow">Act 3 · April 2026 · the budget-control challenge</p>
						<h2>Single-Agent LLMs Outperform MAS Under Equal Thinking Token Budgets</h2>
						<p>
							Tran and Kiela tighten the screws: if MAS gets more visible reasoning
							simply by making more calls, then many claimed architecture gains may
							be unaccounted test-time compute. They compare SAS against five MAS
							variants (Sequential, Subtask-parallel, Parallel-roles, Debate,
							Ensemble) on FRAMES and MuSiQue 4-hop, with the requested
							thinking-token budget matched — 100 to 10,000 tokens — and split
							evenly across a MAS's agents.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="dpi">
						<div class="fig-title">The theory in one line: message passing is a data-processing bottleneck</div>
						<div class="chain">
							<span class="box dim">Y — answer</span>
							<span class="arrow">depends on</span>
							<span class="box">C — full context</span>
							<span class="arrow">g(·) summarize / hand off →</span>
							<span class="box dim">M = g(C) — inter-agent messages</span>
						</div>
						<div class="ineq">I(Y; C) ≥ I(Y; M) &nbsp;⇒&nbsp; P<sub>e</sub>(C) ≤ P<sub>e</sub>(M)</div>
						<p>
							Because messages are a function of the context, the Markov chain
							Y ↔ C ↔ M and the Data Processing Inequality say the messages cannot
							carry <em>more</em> information about the answer than the context
							itself. Under a fixed budget and ideal context use, a single agent
							reading C directly has an information-efficiency advantage. The
							stated assumption — <strong>perfect context utilization</strong> — is
							also the theory's escape hatch: where real models underuse context,
							structured decomposition can re-enter.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="fig">
						<figure>
