/g><text x="96.0" y="87.3" font-size="11" fill="#16202a" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">0.260</text><g><circle cx="328.0" cy="139.3" r="5" fill="#2a78d6" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><title>SAS, substitution α = 0.3: 0.243</title></g><text x="328.0" y="127.3" font-size="11" fill="#16202a" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">0.243</text><g><circle cx="560.0" cy="240.5" r="5" fill="#2a78d6" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><title>SAS, substitution α = 0.7: 0.200</title></g><text x="560.0" y="228.5" font-size="11" fill="#16202a" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">0.200</text><g><circle cx="96.0" cy="172.2" r="5" fill="#1baf7a" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><title>Sequential MAS, clean context: 0.229</title></g><text x="96.0" y="194.2" font-size="11" fill="#596774" text-anchor="middle">0.229</text><g><circle cx="328.0" cy="200.5" r="5" fill="#1baf7a" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><title>Sequential MAS, substitution α = 0.3: 0.217</title></g><text x="328.0" y="222.5" font-size="11" fill="#596774" text-anchor="middle">0.217</text><g><circle cx="560.0" cy="181.6" r="5" fill="#1baf7a" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><title>Sequential MAS, substitution α = 0.7: 0.225</title></g><text x="560.0" y="203.6" font-size="11" fill="#596774" text-anchor="middle">0.225</text><text x="582.0" y="185.6" font-size="11.5" fill="#16202a" text-anchor="start" font-weight="600">MAS wins</text><text x="582.0" y="248.5" font-size="11.5" fill="#596774" text-anchor="start">SAS collapses</text></svg>
							<figcaption>
								Tran &amp; Kiela, Table 13. Under clean context SAS wins comfortably;
								at α = 0.7 substitution, SAS collapses (0.260 → 0.200) while
								Sequential MAS barely moves (0.229 → 0.225) and takes the lead.
								Light corruption and distractor insertion do <em>not</em> flip the
								ranking — only the noise regime that both removes signal and
								injects misleading content does. The open question: do real
								retrieval and long-context failures look like this regime?
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
					</div>

					<p class="takeaway">
						Act 3's discipline: an architecture claim is not interpretable until
						actual compute is reported. Once it is, most MAS gains dissolve — and
						the ones that remain (degraded context, selection at high budget) are
						exactly where the theory says structure should help.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- ============ AGENDA ============ -->
			<section class="block alt" id="agenda">
				<div class="wrap">
					<div class="section-head">
						<p class="eyebrow">Open research direction</p>
						<h2>The next step is not another fixed agent team.</h2>
						<p>
							All three papers point the same way: make orchestration adaptive and
							measured, rather than choosing a template in advance.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="cards">
						<div class="card">
							<h3>Task-conditioned architecture prediction</h3>
							<p>
								Estimate decomposability, sequential dependency, context noise, and
								verifier availability before spending the budget. The target is not
								a MAS/SAS label but expected performance change per unit of
								coordination cost — Kim et al.'s 87% selection accuracy is the
								existence proof.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="card">
							<h3>Trace-level causal diagnostics</h3>
							<p>
								Final accuracy is too coarse. Label where errors enter the graph —
								planning, tool calls, message compression, aggregation,
								verification, stopping — the way Gao et al.'s node/edge/path
								taxonomy and confidence-weighted tracing begin to.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="card">
							<h3>Verifier-centered design</h3>
							<p>
								The most useful second agent is often not a second solver but a
								calibrated verifier placed before irreversible compression. A
								learned orchestrator needs three online estimates: P(single pass
								fails), P(coordination corrects it), and the cost of the
								correction.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

